        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;The annotation symbol &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt; is used to mark confusing sections of a text that need further review. This helps readers identify areas where they need clarification when they return to the text.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="66479_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Marking an important point worth remembering is represented by the symbol &lt;em&gt;! &lt;/em&gt;not &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt;. The &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt; symbol is specifically used to flag confusing sections.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="2624_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Marking a connection to another concept is represented by the symbol &lt;em&gt;C&lt;/em&gt; not &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt;. The &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt; symbol is specifically used to flag confusing sections.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="33487_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Marking a definition of a new term is represented by the symbol &lt;em&gt;D&lt;/em&gt; not &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt;. The &lt;em&gt;?&lt;/em&gt; symbol is specifically used to flag confusing sections.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
    </item>
